WEBVTT

00:00:02.259 --> 00:00:07.860
<v Sarah Okafor>Morning, everyone. Friday the second of October. Can you all hear me? Right, let's get going.</v>

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:15.349
<v Fiona Doyle>Yeah. Before we start, there's a birthday cake in the office. Under the enormous plastic lid.</v>

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:20.089
<v Callum Reid>Ah, that's what that is. Thought someone had brought a sample in.</v>

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:25.640
<v Fiona Doyle>Please don't put a sample label on my cake.</v>

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.530
<v Tomasz Nowak>Anyone doing anything this weekend? We were going walking, but the weather looks properly miserable.</v>

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:39.009
<v Aisha Begum>October walking. That's just getting wet somewhere with no roof, isn't it?</v>

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:46.020
<v Tomasz Nowak>Yeah, apparently it's a hobby. An expensive one if you count the waterproofs.</v>

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:53.549
<v Marcus Hale>I'd settle for a coffee. The broken coffee machine in the canteen has defeated me again.</v>

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:59.079
<v Hannah Price>Still giving you half a cup of warm disappointment?</v>

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:05.409
<v Marcus Hale>Not even half now. That's just me moaning, by the way, not offering to fix it.</v>

00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:12.680
<v Sarah Okafor>Fair enough. Couple of things already closed: tested the line 1 TCU yesterday and it's all fine now.</v>

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.680
<v Callum Reid>Good, that was still showing in the old handover notes.</v>

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.790
<v Sarah Okafor>And already swapped the filter on the waste heat boiler yesterday. That's finished as well.</v>

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.120
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, that one's done. No outstanding filter change on that.</v>

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:35.709
<v Hannah Price>While we're on finished stuff, tested the turbine two yesterday and it's all fine now.</v>

00:01:37.209 --> 00:01:41.060
<v Aisha Begum>That's the result you had at the end of the shift?</v>

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.840
<v Hannah Price>Yep, same result. Closed, not another test request.</v>

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:49.030
<v Sarah Okafor>Lovely. What's actually stopping us this morning?</v>

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.250
<v Callum Reid>The pre-use checks on the FLTs haven't been done. Production's waiting on those.</v>

00:01:56.629 --> 00:02:02.579
<v Hannah Price>I'll do the pre-use checks on the FLTs. That's holding production up so it needs doing first.</v>

00:02:03.879 --> 00:02:05.980
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Hannah. Agreed, those checks first.</v>

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:11.469
<v Hannah Price>Yeah, I'll take those. Don't want that disappearing underneath the rest of the list.</v>

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.639
<v Callum Reid>Cheers. That's the immediate hold-up from our side.</v>

00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:24.189
<v Sarah Okafor>Fiona Doyle, can you organise the toolbox talk for the team by the end of the week? It's fairly important.</v>

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.020
<v Fiona Doyle>Um, yes. Organise it, rather than me being down to deliver it as well?</v>

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.240
<v Sarah Okafor>That's right, just organise the toolbox talk for the team.</v>

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:40.159
<v Fiona Doyle>Okay, I'll organise it by the end of the week. Fairly important, understood.</v>

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:44.830
<v Aisha Begum>Friday already. This week's gone ridiculously quickly.</v>

00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:48.569
<v Fiona Doyle>The calendar's been quite aggressive, yeah.</v>

00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:55.550
<v Sarah Okafor>Marcus Hale, can you sort the permit to work and isolation for the IM2 today? Normal priority.</v>

00:02:56.099 --> 00:03:00.650
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, I'll sort the permit to work and isolation for the IM2 today.</v>

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:06.099
<v Aisha Begum>Sorry, you clipped out for me. Was that normal priority?</v>

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.020
<v Sarah Okafor>Normal priority, yes. Still today.</v>

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:14.789
<v Marcus Hale>Got it. Both the permit and the isolation are with me.</v>

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.930
<v Marcus Hale>And I'll swap the filter on the water booster by the 28th. That's fairly important.</v>

00:03:23.719 --> 00:03:26.870
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Marcus. The water booster filter, by the 28th.</v>

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.180
<v Marcus Hale>Yep. Fairly important, but that is the date for it.</v>

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:38.009
<v Tomasz Nowak>Sorry, talking over you—different filter from the waste heat boiler one we just closed?</v>

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:44.180
<v Marcus Hale>Different one, yeah. Water booster is the one I'm taking on. Waste heat boiler was yesterday.</v>

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:53.210
<v Marcus Hale>While I've got the mic, I'll replace the seals on the main water valve next week. Normal priority for that.</v>

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:59.300
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, Marcus Hale for the seals on the main water valve, next week, normal priority.</v>

00:04:00.789 --> 00:04:01.990
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, that's mine.</v>

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.750
<v Callum Reid>Okay, so the seals aren't part of what's holding us this morning.</v>

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.659
<v Marcus Hale>No, separate job. Next week, as I said.</v>

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:21.860
<v Tomasz Nowak>Just an observation from me: saw the pressure on the line one HPU dip for a few minutes, it came back on its own.</v>

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.079
<v Aisha Begum>Came back without anyone touching it?</v>

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:33.569
<v Tomasz Nowak>Yeah, on its own. Only mentioning what I saw, not putting a repair on today's list.</v>

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:41.870
<v Hannah Price>Similar observation here. Saw the pressure on the Boiler Feedwater Pump dip for a few minutes, it came back on its own.</v>

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:47.290
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, both transient observations, then. Neither's being raised as a new job here.</v>

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.569
<v Hannah Price>Exactly. And I'm not saying they're connected, just that mine recovered too.</v>

00:04:55.370 --> 00:05:02.370
<v Fiona Doyle>On the blocked list, we still can't get to the Injection Moulding Line 3 until production gives us a window.</v>

00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:07.790
<v Callum Reid>Yeah, there's no window agreed. I haven't got one to offer in this meeting.</v>

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:12.639
<v Fiona Doyle>Okay. That stays blocked, then, not going into today's committed work.</v>

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:20.529
<v Tomasz Nowak>Same story for mine. Can't get to the Line 1 Mould Temperature Controller until production gives us a window.</v>

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:26.279
<v Sarah Okafor>Understood. That remains blocked as well; we're not promising access we haven't got.</v>

00:05:28.459 --> 00:05:33.360
<v Tomasz Nowak>Exactly. It's on there for visibility, not because I can suddenly get at it.</v>

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:41.319
<v Aisha Begum>Right, that makes sense. The old handover had the finished bits and blocked bits all mixed together.</v>

00:05:43.079 --> 00:05:48.329
<v Sarah Okafor>Yeah, easy to read it as one giant live list. Anything else we haven't covered?</v>

00:05:50.569 --> 00:05:51.970
<v Callum Reid>Nothing else from production.</v>

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:59.300
<v Fiona Doyle>Nothing from me. Apart from the cake being actual cake, for the avoidance of doubt.</v>

00:05:59.649 --> 00:06:02.800
<v Tomasz Nowak>Best bit of quality assurance we've had all morning.</v>

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.009
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, on that technical note, we're done. Thanks, everyone.</v>
